            {"id": "b", "text": "Type 1 hypervisor; it provides lower overhead and better performance because there is no host OS consuming resources", "correct": True, "rationale": "Correct. ESXi is a Type 1 (bare-metal) hypervisor. Because there is no general-purpose host OS layer between the hypervisor and the hardware, there is less overhead, better hardware access, and higher performance — the primary advantage of Type 1 over Type 2."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "Type 2 hypervisor; it allows multiple host OSes to run simultaneously", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Type 2 hypervisors run on top of a single host OS (e.g., Windows or Linux) and do not inherently support multiple host OSes. ESXi is Type 1, not Type 2."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "Type 1 hypervisor; it is better suited for developer laptops because it requires a host OS for driver support", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Type 1 hypervisors do NOT require a host OS — that is the defining characteristic of Type 2. Developer laptop suitability is actually an advantage of Type 2 hypervisors like VMware Workstation or VirtualBox."},
