            {"id": "b", "text": "Host-only networking", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Host-only networking allows the VM to communicate with the host machine and other VMs on the same host-only network, but blocks all external Internet access. The scenario requires Internet access via NAT while blocking host/VM communication — the opposite configuration."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "NAT networking", "correct": True, "rationale": "Correct. In NAT mode, the VM accesses external networks through the hypervisor's built-in NAT, getting Internet access while being isolated behind NAT from the host and other VMs (which cannot initiate connections to the VM). The VM has no direct layer-2 visibility to the host or peer VMs."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "Internal networking", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Internal networking (available in some hypervisors like VirtualBox) only allows communication between VMs on the same internal network segment — there is no host communication and no Internet access, which does not match the requirement for Internet access via NAT."},
